<section
  class="section text-white"
  id="what-is-higa"
  style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #6e168f, #2a093f);"
>
  <div class="shell grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[0.75fr_1fr] gap-12 lg:gap-24 items-center">
    <img
      src="/assets/images/higa-amulet.jpg"
      alt="The Higa amulet"
      width="1154"
      height="1572"
      loading="lazy"
      class="w-full max-w-[580px] lg:max-w-none max-h-[540px] md:max-h-[620px] lg:max-h-[690px] object-cover rounded-[90px_4px_4px_4px] sm:rounded-[140px_4px_4px_4px] reveal"
    />
    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="kicker text-pink-soft">The symbol</p>
      <h2>What is<br /><span>Higa?</span></h2>
      <div class="text-white/70 text-[17px] leading-[1.8] space-y-4">
        <p>
          The Higa amulet, also known as the Mano Figa, is a protective charm shaped like a closed fist. For centuries, people have worn it to ward off the evil eye.
        </p>
        <p>
          Medieval pilgrims often carried the Higa along the Camino de Santiago for spiritual protection. For women, it also came to represent empowerment and a connection to feminine spiritual strength.
        </p>
        <p>
          We chose this amulet to honour its history and its enduring meaning for pilgrims and women focused on cultivating inner strength and perseverance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
